A||||5||artherd||Bolts and things.||||3||
Z||000000||artherd||08-25-2001||07:44 PM||||So guys, when you break, or more often, find a rusted, bolt...  what do you replace it with?  I'm speaking of the big 'uns now, such as the suspension bushing bolts, or cradle bolts, or bellhousing-engine bolts.<P>Ie, my engine cradle needs new bolts, I just don't trust them anymore.    I belive most fiero bolts are grade 12 hardened steel, and I can't just walk into a hardware store for that kind of fastener :)<P>I would like to use high stregenth stainless (or any non corroding, eg Ti) parts in the Northstar swap's re-assembley., are these available?<P>Rust blows.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000001||theogre||08-25-2001||08:03 PM||||You can use "off the shelf" grade 8, or whatever the metric equivalent is, bolts for most of it. You should be able to find many of the bolts or something very close in the hardware department at most parts stores. MotorMite puts allot of it in blister packs. Some stores have them loose in bins.<P>Unless the bolts are seriously worn, corroded, or have been heated, Grade 8 bolts will tear out of the body pannel before they will break.<P>Check the heads of the bolts against the tables in Haynes or what ever. Unless the bolts are horribly corroded the grade marks may still be there. don't hold your breath tho... GM is great for using unmarked bolts.<P>Grade 12? probably not. Not even head bolts are that hard. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Sleeper SE||08-25-2001||08:12 PM||||if you have a caterpillar dealer near by they have plated metric bolts all grade 8||||reg||1
Z||000003||Devotshka||08-25-2001||09:56 PM||||There is a business in my town that sells nothing but bolts and bearings (brilliantly named "Bolt & Bearing"), i've gotten many bolts replaced there that you just couldn't find anywhere else.  Might we worth looking into if you have something similar nearby, that place comes in handy quite often when working on old cars  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||LS1swap||08-25-2001||11:49 PM||||Try <A HREF="http://www.fastenal.com" TARGET=_blank>www.fastenal.com</A>  they have every bolt in every grade you can think of fortunately for me they have a store in my town so there are no  shipping charges for cash accounts. they say $5.00 min order but they have never held me to it, but like others have said with the exception of flywheel bolts you will be hard pressed to find anything needing more than grade 8 on a fiero||||reg||1
Z||000005||2birds||08-25-2001||11:57 PM||||I've found Lowe's to be about the best for non-hardened type stuff, like bracket holders, exhaust, screws, washers, etc.  Their stainless section is well organized.  Eagle hardware was pretty good back when I was in the Pac Northwest.  <BR>Pep Boys has a selection of larger chassis spec metric bolts, but they aren't stainless.||||reg||1
